#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696649 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x+y}{\\sqrt{2y+x-2}}$." :question/worked-solution "The numerator is a polynomial. The denominator radicand must be nonnegative and its square root must be nonzero, so the radicand must be strictly positive.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y+x-2&>0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 2y+x>2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,2y+x>2\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696655 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x-y^2+1}}{2y^3-x-1}$." :question/worked-solution "The numerator radicand must be nonnegative, and the polynomial denominator must be nonzero. A zero numerator is allowed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx-y^2+1&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x-y^2\\ge-1\\\\\n2y^3-x-1&\\ne 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 2y^3-x\\ne1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,x-y^2\\ge-1,\\ 2y^3-x\\ne1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696672 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2-9}-2}$." :question/worked-solution "Both radicands must be nonnegative. The complete shifted denominator must also be nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ge0\\\\\nx^2+y^2-9&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2\\ge9\\\\\n\\sqrt{x^2+y^2-9}-2&=0\\\\\n\\sqrt{x^2+y^2-9}&=2\\\\\nx^2+y^2-9&=4\\\\\nx^2+y^2&=13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right side of the root equation is positive, so squaring is reversible. Exclude this level set. The radicand-zero boundary remains allowed wherever the numerator is real, since the denominator there is $-2$.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,\\begin{aligned}y &\\ge0\\\\x^2+y^2 &\\ge9\\\\x^2+y^2 &\\ne13\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696673 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{0-\\left(y-x\\right)}}{\\sqrt{9-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)}+1}$." :question/worked-solution "Both radicands must be nonnegative. The complete shifted denominator must also be nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0-\\left(y-x\\right)&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y-x\\le0\\\\\n9-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y^2+x^2\\le9\\\\\n\\sqrt{9-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)}+1&\\ge 1>0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA principal square root is nonnegative. This denominator is always positive on the real-radicand region, so it has no zero to exclude. Squaring an equation with a negative right side would create extraneous points.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y-x\\le0,\\ y^2+x^2\\le9\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696675 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y^2+x^2-9}+2}$." :question/worked-solution "Both radicands must be nonnegative. The complete shifted denominator must also be nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ge0\\\\\ny^2+x^2-9&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y^2+x^2\\ge9\\\\\n\\sqrt{y^2+x^2-9}+2&\\ge 2>0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA principal square root is nonnegative. This denominator is always positive on the real-radicand region, so it has no zero to exclude. Squaring an equation with a negative right side would create extraneous points.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y\\ge0,\\ y^2+x^2\\ge9\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696692 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{0-\\left(y-x\\right)}}{\\sqrt{4-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)}-1}$." :question/worked-solution "Both radicands must be nonnegative. The complete shifted denominator must also be nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0-\\left(y-x\\right)&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y-x\\le0\\\\\n4-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y^2+x^2\\le4\\\\\n\\sqrt{4-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)}-1&=0\\\\\n\\sqrt{4-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)}&=1\\\\\n4-\\left(y^2+x^2\\right)&=1\\\\\ny^2+x^2&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right side of the root equation is positive, so squaring is reversible. Exclude this level set. The radicand-zero boundary remains allowed wherever the numerator is real, since the denominator there is $-1$.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,\\begin{aligned}y-x &\\le0\\\\y^2+x^2 &\\le4\\\\y^2+x^2 &\\ne3\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696715 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the real-valued function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x - y - 1}}$?" :question/worked-solution "The radicand must be nonnegative and the denominator must be nonzero. Together these require a strictly positive radicand.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&x - y - 1>0 \\\\ &\\Longleftrightarrow y<x - 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ gives $y<x - 1$. Shade **below** the boundary $y=x - 1$. The boundary is **excluded** (dashed).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\;:\\;y<x - 1\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9b/9b36c2590695b16770b794827fbd2c9aca7e752ae9478fcef8ceb02c218fd1c1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696722 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the real-valued function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{y - \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} + 2}$?" :question/worked-solution "The radicand must be nonnegative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&y - \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} + 2\\ge 0 \\\\ &\\Longleftrightarrow y\\ge \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ gives $y\\ge \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} - 2$. Shade **above** the boundary $y=\\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} - 2$. The boundary is **included** (solid).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\;:\\;y\\ge \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} - 2\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/c8/c8007a2f28c43a79497c56758e098bc98d04e4a4336fe7e28fab2288165b499b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696726 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the real-valued function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{- x^{2} + y^{2}}}$?" :question/worked-solution "The radicand must be nonnegative and the denominator must be nonzero. Together these require a strictly positive radicand.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&- x^{2} + y^{2}>0 \\\\ &\\Longleftrightarrow x^2<y^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the squares gives $x^2<y^2$. Shade the **upper and lower** wedges between the diagonal lines $y=x$ and $y=-x$. The boundary is **excluded** (dashed).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\;:\\;x^2<y^2\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/da/dabf2ddfb2c1d9e72bc1e4fe398d078cdb70e7d9ba52c7ddfe470ac08bcb41f8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696734 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the real-valued function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} - 9}}$?" :question/worked-solution "The radicand must be nonnegative and the denominator must be nonzero. Together these require a strictly positive radicand.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&x^{2} + y^{2} - 9>0 \\\\ &\\Longleftrightarrow x^{2} + y^{2}>9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearrange to obtain $x^{2} + y^{2}>9$. The boundary is centered at $(0,0)$ with radius $3$. Shade **outside** it. The boundary is **excluded** (dashed).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\;:\\;x^{2} + y^{2}>9\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/0b/0b72e9ceedd654dc265b6bb84b5abc4e17a5fd865d210edc2e70cfa587bee674.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696742 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the real-valued function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + 2}$?" :question/worked-solution "The radicand must be nonnegative. Both squared terms are nonnegative, so $\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + 2\\ge 2>0$ for every real pair $(x,y)$. Therefore $D=\\mathbb{R}^2$: shade the **entire plane**.\n\n![](images/dd/dd23edd161f65e0bcabfca0b116ce3723bd0655256bdd14ce83d91027fca9513.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696748 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the real-valued function $f(x,y)=\\ln\\!\\left(- x^{2} - 9 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 9\\right)$?" :question/worked-solution "The argument of the logarithm must be positive.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&- x^{2} - 9 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 9>0 \\\\ &\\Longleftrightarrow \\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2}<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearrange and divide by the positive constant $9$ to obtain $\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2}<1$. The boundary is centered at $(0,-1)$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $1$. Shade **inside** it. The boundary is **excluded** (dashed).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\;:\\;\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2}<1\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f80443f846d10567795f4d411ad9fdf308c07d131926622182c6b491e3557222.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696765 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the real-valued function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{\\ln\\!\\left(x - \\frac{y}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)}$?" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm needs a positive argument, and its value in the denominator must be nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x - \\frac{y}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}&>0 \\\\ \\ln\\!\\left(x - \\frac{y}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)&\\ne0 \\\\ x - \\frac{y}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}&\\ne1 \\\\ 2 x - y + 1&\\ne 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\ln(1)=0$ and the logarithm is one-to-one on positive inputs, its argument must differ from $1$. Multiplying both restrictions by the positive constant $2$ gives $2 x - y + 1>0,\\quad 2 x - y + 1\\ne 2$. Shade the half-plane $2 x - y + 1>0$ and remove the parallel line $2 x - y + 1=2$. Both lines are **excluded**, so draw them dashed. Keep both portions of the positive half-plane separated by the removed line.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\;:\\;\\begin{aligned}2 x - y + 1&>0 \\\\ 2 x - y + 1&\\ne 2\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b1/b1509b409434570f458c3cf057a6737cad16aa4a94e9ccc7f4bdaa904a401ce7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696768 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the real-valued function $f(x,y)=\\ln\\!\\left(9 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 36\\right)$?" :question/worked-solution "The argument of the logarithm must be positive.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&9 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 36>0 \\\\ &\\Longleftrightarrow \\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9}>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearrange and divide by the positive constant $36$ to obtain $\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9}>1$. The boundary is centered at $(0,0)$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3$. Shade **outside** it. The boundary is **excluded** (dashed).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nD=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\;:\\;\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9}>1\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ec/ecc8a5094076a3c9233bd239920e724a3c18206d4146aee01b5f86aa19445e0d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696840 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\ln\\left(x\\right)}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm requires $x>0$.\n\nThe denominator must be nonzero, so $y\\ne 0$.\n\nIntersecting these conditions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x>0 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}:\\,\\begin{aligned}&x>0 \\\\ &y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696858 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y^{2}}{\\ln\\left(-2x^{2}+y-1\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm requires $-2x^{2}+y-1>0$. Its value is a denominator, so it must also be nonzero.\n\nOn positive inputs, $\\ln\\left(-2x^{2}+y-1\\right)=0$ exactly when $-2x^{2}+y-1=1$. Thus we must exclude $-2x^{2}+y-1=1$, even when the numerator is zero.\n\nIntersecting these conditions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x^{2}+y-1>0 \\\\ -2x^{2}+y-1\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}:\\,\\begin{aligned}&-2x^{2}+y-1>0 \\\\ &-2x^{2}+y-1\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696859 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\ln\\left(1-x^{2}\\right)}{y+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm requires $1-x^{2}>0$.\n\nThe denominator must be nonzero, so $y+2\\ne 0$.\n\nIntersecting these conditions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-x^{2}>0 \\\\ y+2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}:\\,\\begin{aligned}&1-x^{2}>0 \\\\ &y+2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696860 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\ln\\left(-x+2y-1\\right)}{e^{x+2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm requires $-x+2y-1>0$.\n\nThe denominator $e^{x+2}$ is positive for every real input, so it never vanishes.\n\nIntersecting these conditions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2y-1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}:\\,-x+2y-1>0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696865 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\ln\\left(-x+2y-2\\right)}{\\sqrt{x+2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm requires $-x+2y-2>0$.\n\nThe denominator square root requires both $x+2\\ge 0$ and $\\sqrt{x+2}\\ne 0$. Together these give $x+2>0$.\n\nIntersecting these conditions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2y-2>0 \\\\ x+2>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}:\\,\\begin{aligned}&-x+2y-2>0 \\\\ &x+2>0\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696874 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\arcsin\\left(x+\\sqrt{y}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The inner square root requires $y\\ge 0$.\n\nThe input of $\\arcsin$ must lie in the closed interval $[-1,1]$, including both endpoints. Therefore $-1\\le x+\\sqrt{y}\\le 1$.\n\nIntersecting these conditions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1\\le x+\\sqrt{y}\\le 1 \\\\ y\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}:\\,\\begin{aligned}&-1\\le x+\\sqrt{y}\\le 1 \\\\ &y\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696879 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\cdot \\sin\\left(y\\right)\n$$\n\nat the point $(-2,\\frac{\\pi}{4})$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=-2$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(-2,\\frac{\\pi}{4})=(-2)\\cdot \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n(-2)\\cdot \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) &=- \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(-2,\\frac{\\pi}{4})=- \\sqrt{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696881 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt{x\\cdot {y}^{2}+2}\n$$\n\nat the point $(-2,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=-2$ and $y=1$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(-2,1)=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot {1}^{2}+2}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{1}^{2} &=1 \\\\\n(-2)\\cdot {1}^{2} &=-2 \\\\\n(-2)\\cdot {1}^{2}+2 &=0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot {1}^{2}+2} &=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(-2,1)=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696882 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2\\cdot \\arctan\\left(y\\right)-x\n$$\n\nat the point $(\\pi,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=\\pi$ and $y=-1$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(\\pi,-1)=2\\cdot \\arctan\\left((-1)\\right)-\\pi\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arctan\\left((-1)\\right) &=- \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\\\\n2\\cdot \\arctan\\left((-1)\\right) &=- \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\\\\n2\\cdot \\arctan\\left((-1)\\right)-\\pi &=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(\\pi,-1)=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696890 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x\\cdot y+8}\n$$\n\nat the point $(-3,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=-3$ and $y=3$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(-3,3)=\\sqrt[3]{(-3)\\cdot 3+8}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)\\cdot 3 &=-9 \\\\\n(-3)\\cdot 3+8 &=-1 \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{(-3)\\cdot 3+8} &=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(-3,3)=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696908 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\arcsin\\left(x\\right)}\n$$\n\nat the point $(1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(1,2)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{\\arcsin\\left(1\\right)}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{e}^{2} &=e^{2} \\\\\n\\arcsin\\left(1\\right) &=\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{{e}^{2}}{\\arcsin\\left(1\\right)} &=\\frac{2 e^{2}}{\\pi}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(1,2)=\\frac{2 e^{2}}{\\pi}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696940 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{\\log_{3}\\left({x}^{2}+y-6\\right)}{\\sqrt{x-y+10}}\n$$\n\nat the point $(2,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(2,3)=\\frac{\\log_{3}\\left({2}^{2}+3-6\\right)}{\\sqrt{2-3+10}}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{2}^{2} &=4 \\\\\n{2}^{2}+3 &=7 \\\\\n{2}^{2}+3-6 &=1 \\\\\n\\log_{3}\\left({2}^{2}+3-6\\right) &=0 \\\\\n2-3 &=-1 \\\\\n2-3+10 &=9 \\\\\n\\sqrt{2-3+10} &=3 \\\\\n\\frac{\\log_{3}\\left({2}^{2}+3-6\\right)}{\\sqrt{2-3+10}} &=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(2,3)=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696941 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{\\log_{2}\\left(x\\cdot {y}^{2}\\right)+\\sqrt{x-y+6}}{{2}^{(-1)\\cdot y}-x+0}\n$$\n\nat the point $(1,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=1$ and $y=-2$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(1,-2)=\\frac{\\log_{2}\\left(1\\cdot {(-2)}^{2}\\right)+\\sqrt{1-(-2)+6}}{{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-2)}-1+0}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{(-2)}^{2} &=4 \\\\\n1\\cdot {(-2)}^{2} &=4 \\\\\n\\log_{2}\\left(1\\cdot {(-2)}^{2}\\right) &=2 \\\\\n1-(-2) &=3 \\\\\n1-(-2)+6 &=9 \\\\\n\\sqrt{1-(-2)+6} &=3 \\\\\n\\log_{2}\\left(1\\cdot {(-2)}^{2}\\right)+\\sqrt{1-(-2)+6} &=5 \\\\\n(-1)\\cdot (-2) &=2 \\\\\n{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-2)} &=4 \\\\\n{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-2)}-1 &=3 \\\\\n{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-2)}-1+0 &=3 \\\\\n\\frac{\\log_{2}\\left(1\\cdot {(-2)}^{2}\\right)+\\sqrt{1-(-2)+6}}{{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-2)}-1+0} &=\\frac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(1,-2)=\\frac{5}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696946 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{\\log_{2}\\left(x\\cdot {y}^{2}\\right)+\\sqrt{x-y-5}}{{2}^{(-1)\\cdot y}-x+0}\n$$\n\nat the point $(4,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=4$ and $y=-1$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(4,-1)=\\frac{\\log_{2}\\left(4\\cdot {(-1)}^{2}\\right)+\\sqrt{4-(-1)-5}}{{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-1)}-4+0}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{(-1)}^{2} &=1 \\\\\n4\\cdot {(-1)}^{2} &=4 \\\\\n\\log_{2}\\left(4\\cdot {(-1)}^{2}\\right) &=2 \\\\\n4-(-1) &=5 \\\\\n4-(-1)-5 &=0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{4-(-1)-5} &=0 \\\\\n\\log_{2}\\left(4\\cdot {(-1)}^{2}\\right)+\\sqrt{4-(-1)-5} &=2 \\\\\n(-1)\\cdot (-1) &=1 \\\\\n{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-1)} &=2 \\\\\n{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-1)}-4 &=-2 \\\\\n{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-1)}-4+0 &=-2 \\\\\n\\frac{\\log_{2}\\left(4\\cdot {(-1)}^{2}\\right)+\\sqrt{4-(-1)-5}}{{2}^{(-1)\\cdot (-1)}-4+0} &=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(4,-1)=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696948 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{\\log_{3}\\left({x}^{2}+y- \\frac{62}{9}\\right)}{\\sqrt{x-y+5}}\n$$\n\nat the point $(-2,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(-2,3)=\\frac{\\log_{3}\\left({(-2)}^{2}+3- \\frac{62}{9}\\right)}{\\sqrt{(-2)-3+5}}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{(-2)}^{2} &=4 \\\\\n{(-2)}^{2}+3 &=7 \\\\\n{(-2)}^{2}+3- \\frac{62}{9} &=\\frac{1}{9} \\\\\n\\log_{3}\\left({(-2)}^{2}+3- \\frac{62}{9}\\right) &=-2 \\\\\n(-2)-3 &=-5 \\\\\n(-2)-3+5 &=0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-2)-3+5} &=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivision by zero is undefined. Therefore, the function is **undefined** at this point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696960 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x-y+10}}{x+y-4}\n$$\n\nat the point $(2,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ throughout the expression. Keep parentheses around signed inputs:\n\n$$\nf(2,3)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2-3+10}}{2+3-4}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the inner operations before combining them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2-3 &=-1 \\\\\n2-3+10 &=9 \\\\\n\\sqrt{2-3+10} &=3 \\\\\n2+3 &=5 \\\\\n2+3-4 &=1 \\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{2-3+10}}{2+3-4} &=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(2,3)=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697056 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}+2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}+2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha} \\\\ &=2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\alpha}+\\cos^{2}{\\alpha}) \\\\ &=2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cdot 1 \\\\ &=2\\sin{\\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&2\\sin^{3}{\\alpha}+2\\sin{\\alpha}\\cos^{2}{\\alpha} \\\\ &=2\\sin{\\alpha}\\end{aligned}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697078 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-4\\sin^{3}{x}-4\\sin{x}\\cos^{2}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&-4\\sin^{3}{x}-4\\sin{x}\\cos^{2}{x} \\\\ &=-4\\sin{x}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{x}+\\cos^{2}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{x}+\\cos^{2}{x}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&-4\\sin{x}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{x}+\\cos^{2}{x}) \\\\ &=-4\\sin{x}\\cdot 1 \\\\ &=-4\\sin{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&-4\\sin^{3}{x}-4\\sin{x}\\cos^{2}{x} \\\\ &=-4\\sin{x}\\end{aligned}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697079 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-3\\sin^{2}{x}\\cos{x}-3\\cos^{3}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&-3\\sin^{2}{x}\\cos{x}-3\\cos^{3}{x} \\\\ &=-3\\cos{x}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{x}+\\cos^{2}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{x}+\\cos^{2}{x}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&-3\\cos{x}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{x}+\\cos^{2}{x}) \\\\ &=-3\\cos{x}\\cdot 1 \\\\ &=-3\\cos{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&-3\\sin^{2}{x}\\cos{x}-3\\cos^{3}{x} \\\\ &=-3\\cos{x}\\end{aligned}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697081 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $5\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}+5\\sin{\\theta}\\cos^{3}{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&5\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}+5\\sin{\\theta}\\cos^{3}{\\theta} \\\\ &=5\\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&5\\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta}) \\\\ &=5\\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}\\cdot 1 \\\\ &=5\\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&5\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}+5\\sin{\\theta}\\cos^{3}{\\theta} \\\\ &=5\\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}\\end{aligned}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697083 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $-\\sin^{5}{\\theta}-\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cos^{2}{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&-\\sin^{5}{\\theta}-\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cos^{2}{\\theta} \\\\ &=-\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&-\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^{2}{\\theta}+\\cos^{2}{\\theta}) \\\\ &=-\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cdot 1 \\\\ &=-\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&-\\sin^{5}{\\theta}-\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\cos^{2}{\\theta} \\\\ &=-\\sin^{3}{\\theta}\\end{aligned}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697156 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(2x+1)^3$, find $f'(x)$.\n\nUse the decomposition $g(x)=2x+1$ and $F(u)=u^3$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$. Recall that $f'(x)=F'(g(x))g'(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the outer and inner functions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF'(u)&=3u^2, \\\\\ng'(x)&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe chain rule multiplies the outer derivative, evaluated at the unchanged inner expression, by the inner derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)&=F'(g(x))g'(x) \\\\\n&=3(2x+1)^2\\cdot 2 \\\\\n&=6(2x+1)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697162 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x^2-3x+3}}$, complete the expression for $f'(x)$ by entering the missing factor.\n\n$f'(x)=\\dfrac{1}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}}\\,($ {{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=x^2-3x+3$ and rewrite the outer function as $F(u)=2u^{-1/2}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiate the outer and inner functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF'(u)&=2\\left(-\\frac12\\right)u^{-3/2}=-u^{-3/2}, \\\\\ng'(x)&=2x-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)&=F'(g(x))g'(x) \\\\\n&=-(x^2-3x+3)^{-3/2}(2x-3) \\\\\n&=\\frac{3-2x}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}}(3-2x).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing factor is $3-2x$. It includes both the inner derivative and the outer derivative's factor of $-1$.\n\nSince $x^2-3x+3=(x-\\frac32)^2+\\frac34>0$, this derivative is defined for every real $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697163 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(3-2x)^4$, find $f'(x)$.\n\n$f'(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=3-2x$ and $F(u)=u^4$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiate each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF'(u)&=4u^3, \\\\\ng'(x)&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the chain rule and substitute $g(x)=3-2x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)&=F'(g(x))g'(x) \\\\\n&=4(3-2x)^3\\cdot(-2) \\\\\n&=-8(3-2x)^3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inner derivative supplies the negative sign; the inner expression itself stays $3-2x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697642 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2t-1,\\qquad y=t^2+1,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nFill in the three blanks. Use the value of $t$ at the top of each column in the formula for that row. For example, the first entry in the $x$-row is $x(-2)=2(-2)-1=-5$.\n\n| $t$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-5$ | {{field-1}} | $-1$ | $1$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $y$ | {{field-3}} | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The row tells us which formula to use, and the column tells us the value of $t$.\n\nFor the $x$-entry under $t=-1$, substitute $-1$ into $x=2t-1$:\n\n$$\nx(-1)=2(-1)-1=-2-1=-3.\n$$\n\nFor the $x$-entry under $t=2$, use the same formula:\n\n$$\nx(2)=2(2)-1=4-1=3.\n$$\n\nFor the $y$-entry under $t=-2$, use $y=t^2+1$. Square the entire negative number:\n\n$$\ny(-2)=(-2)^2+1=4+1=5.\n$$\n\nThe completed table is\n\n| $t$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697645 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right),\\qquad y=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right),\\qquad -3\\le t\\le 4,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values. Angles are in radians.\n\n| $t$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $b$ | $1$ | $c$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nFind the exact value of $a-b+c$." :question/worked-solution "Read each letter's row and column before substituting.\n\nThe entry $a$ is an $x$-value at $t=-3$:\n\n$$\na=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi(-3)}{4}\\right)=\\sin\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHere $\\sin(-\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta$ and $\\sin(3\\pi/4)=\\sqrt{2}/2$.\n\nThe entry $b$ is a $y$-value at $t=-2$:\n\n$$\nb=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi(-2)}{6}\\right)=\\cos\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nCosine is even, so $\\cos(-\\pi/3)=\\cos(\\pi/3)$.\n\nThe entry $c$ is a $y$-value at $t=1$:\n\n$$\nc=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi(1)}{6}\\right)=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substitute these three entries into the requested expression, keeping the subtraction before $b$:\n\n$$\na-b+c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe completed table is\n\n| $t$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |"}]}
